 ## Subtopics
 
-### [Virtual Multi-Resolution Volume Address Translation](16088-virtual-multi-resolution-volume-address-translation.md)
-Virtual Multi-Resolution Volume Address Translation is a method that maps virtual multi-resolution volume coordinates to physical addresses in a GPU block cache using a page table hierarchy. It solves the problem of addressing anisotropic down-sampled volume data by packing multiple resolution levels into a single 3D texture, enabling efficient memory management for petascale microscopy data streams. It builds on [Hierarchical Index/Address Translation](8371-hierarchical-index-address-translation.md) by providing the specific translation logic and data layout for multi-resolution page directories.
+### Virtual Multi-Resolution Volume Address Translation
+A method to translate a virtual multi-resolution address $(l, p)$ to a physical address in the block cache using a page table hierarchy [S2]. It handles anisotropic down-sampling by packing resolution levels into a single 3D texture `texPageDir` [S2]. Choose this when rendering multi-resolution volumes with anisotropic down-sampling where regular mipmaps are insufficient [S2].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Not given in the sources.
-*   **Memory Complexity:** The method packs bricks into a single 3D texture. There is a 10% overhead for filtering with $32^3$ bricks due to duplicate neighbor voxels [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Measured Performance:** Adaptive volume sampling using this method achieved a speed-up of 2.6-2.8x compared to full density rendering [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Supports multi-resolution rendering; allows arbitrary coordinate transformations without rebinding textures; efficient memory management for large datasets.
-*   **Limitations:** Requires an additional index texture; specific performance characteristics depend on the hardware and resolution.
-*   **When to use:** When rendering large, multi-resolution volume datasets (e.g., petascale microscopy) where memory efficiency and adaptive sampling are critical.
-*   **When not to use:** For simple, single-resolution volumes where the overhead of the index texture is not justified.
+*   **Time Complexity:** Lookup is $O(1)$ per level in the hierarchy, but cache misses incur CPU-GPU transfer latency [S2].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Requires storage for the page directory, page table cache, and block cache, all implemented as 3D textures [S2].
+*   **Performance:** Adaptive volume sampling using this method achieved a speed-up of 2.6-2.8x compared to full density rendering [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** Supports multi-resolution rendering and arbitrary coordinate transformations without rebinding textures [Parent, S1]. Efficiently handles empty space skipping [S2].
+*   **Limitations:** Complex implementation due to custom packing of the page directory and CPU-GPU synchronization for cache management [S2].
 
 ## Background
-The method is described in the context of GPU-based volume ray-casting with advanced illumination techniques [S1, unofficial]. It is part of a broader effort to make volume rendering more flexible and efficient on GPUs, particularly for large-scale scientific data. The approach is linked to adaptive volume sampling strategies that utilize hierarchical levels to optimize rendering speed [S1, unofficial].
+The concept of using a layout/index texture for address translation was introduced in "GPU-Based Volume Ray-Casting with Advanced Illumination" [S1, unofficial]. The hierarchical multi-resolution variant was developed for "Interactive Volume Exploration of Petascale Microscopy Data Streams" to handle large datasets [S2].
 
